  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms remain\npossible until a cold front crosses tonight, with a chance \npossibly persisting into Tuesday.\n\nConvective activity which developed during daytime heating has\nsince pushed down into southern Maryland. These continue to\nproduce frequent lightning strikes, which also includes some\npotential of near-severe caliber wind fields. Although the\nboundary layer is slowly stabilizing, there appears to be enough\nresidual instability to mix some higher winds down to the\nsurface. However, such gusts should be somewhat sporadic and \nisolated in nature.\n\nThe low-level synoptic cold front will cross the region \ntonight. Therefore, a few additional showers are possible, but\nthe threat for thunderstorms should start diminishing after\nmidnight. Areas which clear out tonight could have patchy fog.\nMost guidance favors the western river valleys at this time.\n\nWhile the surface front will likely be south of the area\nTuesday, it will be losing its definition, and some low and mid\nlevel moisture may linger, especially across the southern half\nof the area. With daytime heating, this will result in some weak\ninstability. Some models have scattered showers and storms\ndeveloping off the terrain and moving eastward during the\nafternoon across the southern quarter to half of the forecast\narea (mostly south of I-66 if not I-64). The weak instability\nwill limit the strength of this activity despite shear which\ncould approach 30 kt. Temperatures and dew points will be a\ntouch lower today, but still seasonable with highs in the mid to\nupper 80s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorm chances return Wednesday night into \nThursday, and continue into next weekend.\n\nHigh pressure building from the Ohio Valley will keep dry\nconditions Tuesday night into Wednesday. Temperatures may tick\nback up closer to 90. The next cold front will approach from \nthe northwest Wednesday night, but the timing for rain chances \nhas generally been pushed to the second half of the night, which\nwould reduce the threat for thunderstorms. Where this cold \nfront ends up by Thursday afternoon will have an effect on the \ncoverage and strength of showers and thunderstorms. While\neveryone could see some rain, there is uncertainty with the\namount of instability and how much it would overlap with shear.\nAI/ML guidance has been consistent the past several days \nshowing the greatest risk for any stronger thunderstorms being \nacross the southern or southeastern portion of the forecast \narea.\n\nIt's possible the front will stall or only move just south of \nthe area, which will play a role in precipitation chances \nFriday. It will be far enough south to bring a cool down, with \nhighs in the 70s and 80s by Friday.\n\nA deepening trough of low pressure approaching from the Great\nLakes (possibly even a closed low) may result in one or more\nareas of low pressure developing along the front between Friday\nand this weekend. Thus chances of rain have been trending \nupward, even if we are north of the low track. Expect \ntemperatures slightly below normal given the clouds and \npotential rain.",
